#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055682} :activity/id #uuid "5aea7af6-7507-583d-9ea4-e7afac732f55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050987 :topic/id #uuid "7734519e-e666-5361-81f3-89b86b5bc28d" :topic/math-academy-id 37} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054604 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054518 :question/id #uuid "8177157c-4d89-59df-af9b-38f1bee2d6a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3564"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1bddb74-97be-5f35-b559-afb707e6e964" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049299}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563742}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Root Rule to Compute a Limit"} :step/id #uuid "9450e070-8dcd-5ffd-bf6c-45e46af5913a" :step/math-academy-id 12007 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055686}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054605 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054519 :question/id #uuid "3d6b32ee-3385-5fba-91a5-66c0bbb13dea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3565"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10100e89-21d6-53c5-a7de-f14fd73ce4b7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049089}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514399}] :knowledge-point/title "Combining Different Rules to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "d5acac99-2496-5224-a6b8-9e6864591fd4" :step/math-academy-id 12008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055954 :tutorial/content "The **power rule** states that the limit of a power is the power of the limit. More precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}(f(x))^{n}={({lim}_{x\\to a}f(x))}^{n}={L}^{n}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the power rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}(3x-4)^{8}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within parentheses and then taking the power of the result.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4)^{8} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-4))}^{8} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 2-4)^{8} \\\\  & ={2}^{8} \\\\  & =256\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4331194-9993-526c-ac4e-85c5017ee722" :tutorial/math-academy-id 181 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ea15caf2-d9e3-5bc4-a4eb-aeae23270b0c" :step/math-academy-id 344 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054512 :question/id #uuid "9f9e4a39-22c9-5bcf-9b34-b3b1459acb44" :question/math-academy-id "e-30"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae7650e3-faf3-520c-9674-ffa036a8eacc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049226}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514416}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit"} :step/id #uuid "7cdd9667-323b-5010-a8fc-c007664db1d4" :step/math-academy-id 345 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054703 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054513 :question/id #uuid "27b89a42-7f31-579b-af21-b33ebe36f749" :question/math-academy-id "e-31"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad6d0a4-7854-5f87-896c-0c3ba5ef60af" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563676}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Power Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "2c4ae36c-001f-593c-aad4-04fe90c082c3" :step/math-academy-id 346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055955 :tutorial/content "The **root rule** states that the limit of a root is the root of the limit (provided that the root exists).\n\nMore precisely, if $n$ is any positive integer and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\sqrt[n]{f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{{lim}_{x\\to a}f(x)}=\\sqrt[n]{L}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, with the root rule, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}\\sqrt{3x-2}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of the expression within the root and then taking the root of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{3x-2} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(3x-2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3\\cdot 2-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "831603dd-2fca-539d-90bc-81d866ca5d77" :tutorial/math-academy-id 185 :tutorial/title "The Root Rule for Limits"} :step/id #uuid "feb2aa0a-5b34-5dae-ba30-562950046860" :step/math-academy-id 348 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055518}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055686 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055956 :tutorial/content "If we combine the power rule and the root rule, then we get the following useful generalization: if $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L,$ then for any fraction $p/q,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}\\left(f(x)\\right)^{p/q} =  \\left(\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} f(x)\\right)^{p/q} = L^{p/q}\n$$\n\nprovided that $L^{p/q}$ exists.\n\nFor example, we can evaluate a limit like \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow 64} y^{-1/6}\n$$\n\n by evaluating the limit of $y$ and then exponentiating the result to the fractional exponent:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to 64}{y}^{-1/6} & ={({lim}_{y\\to 64}y)}^{-1/6} \\\\  & =(64)^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={({2}^{6})}^{-1/6} \\\\  & ={2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0ec92465-592d-5e08-8d49-a2a3311282c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 187 :tutorial/title "Combining Different Rules"} :step/id #uuid "4c16f856-e95a-5082-937e-48f3494765ac" :step/math-academy-id 350 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055519}}] :activity/title "The Power and Root Rules for Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054899}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534121} :activity/id #uuid "38e95199-7378-5c57-8b6f-37733a1f504d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050987 :topic/id #uuid "7734519e-e666-5361-81f3-89b86b5bc28d" :topic/math-academy-id 37} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b2d9170-72bf-5608-bc85-b7e1e90b7337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533246} {:answer/id #uuid "3a9642e4-5571-5f69-b42d-8a766a19f94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533276} {:answer/id #uuid "b3087c4f-f3ff-5f73-85e3-80711023e115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533572} {:answer/id #uuid "c97fc178-695e-5644-8659-728e66bdb6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533621} {:answer/id #uuid "cfdaab07-184b-57e0-83cf-49eb37899d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533276} :answer-field/id #uuid "eccb0955-0665-53a1-96c5-95a613de628c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "98c65517-e27d-5eb7-b458-8dcf5425928f" :question/math-academy-id "q-100273" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fda731fa3c9a3401e9b9e2a7d4c103829d3fe3b14ac9d6f2872b76a319f6ae91.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=1.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x)))}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{3}-4(1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2(1)^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{3}-4f(x))^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(2{x}^{4}-3)^{2} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ceee877-c8fc-531a-9878-62234964e115"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f5e618-9958-5c65-98d7-f72834d8cfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052636} {:answer/id #uuid "c02dfdc4-656f-5641-998f-9bc8a0a18e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052637} {:answer/id #uuid "d027fbd4-28ba-5990-9755-63cf439bf5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052638} {:answer/id #uuid "41356929-64d9-5d70-95a1-343710b86dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-641" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052639} {:answer/id #uuid "42e68d28-554b-55d7-b11f-12bfa298f433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052640}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186052640} :answer-field/id #uuid "022ec75f-d363-5486-95b7-fc4dff138c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186052635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0bbcd500-ae70-509c-b570-d85bc1475ae3" :question/math-academy-id "q-35294" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b75586450ddee4f682f02539e02462d0aa5f88830b2989cc7cc2f4f6df635ade.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=0.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4}={lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(5+7\\cdot (-1))^{3} \\\\  & =(-2)^{3} \\\\  & =-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x))}^{4} \\\\  & =(0-(-1))^{4} \\\\  & ={1}^{4} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}(f(x)-x)^{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(5+7x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}(f(x)-x)^{4} \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4c6aa450-6cdc-50b4-806e-6dec30f5eb67" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5797e9e9-6e67-537d-a655-48ab777ada22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533344} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc339c6-f7b7-5c24-ab62-8e1ff9e4ed3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533394} {:answer/id #uuid "df10c16c-70c6-58c4-9182-e668df07e549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533689} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d0edc5-1ae9-515d-a0fc-bd1888788abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533708} {:answer/id #uuid "f40b5418-f57a-574c-a2f2-815b0c4b394d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533394} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0ef0b5-fcba-553e-ac8c-0225ffb723a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "946e7009-82f2-5bbe-a154-157adaaa3621" :question/math-academy-id "q-35295" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e092490e087b5245f05b352b4738beb1ce3d9d649ea289ded881d0728129e5.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to 1/2}f(x)=1.$\n\nApplying the root rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\sqrt{4(f(x))^{4}+10x} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}(4(f(x))^{4}+10x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(1)^{4}+10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ec7c12e6-02c7-5b5a-a366-35e1a8ba03d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534118}}] :activity/title "The Power and Root Rules for Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533121}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563772} :activity/id #uuid "893cd585-763a-5ad4-9659-5052de0c1e02" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050987 :topic/id #uuid "7734519e-e666-5361-81f3-89b86b5bc28d" :topic/math-academy-id 37} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ba1e29-25ac-579d-9cfe-24448c56edab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562995} {:answer/id #uuid "a0bf2b88-34c6-53e4-a455-89e06f7f9a7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563306} {:answer/id #uuid "db467dc8-4b26-512d-821f-e3c4973e9fd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563426} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdaaac8-b49f-51b4-b323-7ea59a4fdd9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563510} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7f4802-e1a5-55e2-9352-05637b9f8392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563516} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddff0f96-aeb7-5490-8d56-4c8b9d0de231" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d591e3b7-0993-5fb9-b7f0-ff97c33bf559" :question/math-academy-id "q-100250" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 2}3x}{{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nFor the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}3x & =3{lim}_{x\\to 2}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the root rule in the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to 2}(2{x}^{4}-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{2(2)^{4}-5)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{3x}{\\sqrt[3]{2{x}^{4}-5}} & =\\frac{6}{3}=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2e1eff63-f8c6-5d02-9662-7565ab1c3899" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563794}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563787 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d434ed0-8529-5bae-a087-2963bb7442d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562969} :answer-field/id #uuid "32d33a71-45d3-5a70-b6f2-f587f0b3ec0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b9acb7e-8235-5df0-907d-863119079541" :question/math-academy-id "q-338390" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n${lim}_{t\\to -1}(\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and root rules, we get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to -1}(\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}}) & ={lim}_{t\\to -1}\\sqrt[7]{(2t+1)^{7}}-{lim}_{t\\to -1}\\sqrt[3]{({t}^{2}+t+1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{{({lim}_{t\\to -1}(2t+1))}^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{{({lim}_{t\\to -1}({t}^{2}+t+1))}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(2(-1)+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{(((-1)^{2}+(-1)+1))^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(-2+1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{((1-1+1))^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{(-1)^{7}}-\\sqrt[3]{(1)^{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[7]{-1}-\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "75c9d97b-2b75-5de2-b1d4-d07fb1dfbf30"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066cb984-e17a-5fea-af4c-48188475d862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562952} :answer-field/id #uuid "817fc64f-fe17-5ab1-8ab5-8f8ebfd722a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41ad1c03-af11-5517-b3f9-c73f27dc13ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-338364" :question/problem "![](images/94/94027c25ef78ae46d0deb4b577339daf878efd71b71029a9575f9c8ed9096e2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the function $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=3.$\n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3}={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1))}^{2} \\\\  & =(2-1)^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(f(x)-2))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3-2)^{3} \\\\  & ={1}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,2}(x-1)^{2}\\,(f(x)-2)^{3} & =(1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9a7d063a-cc3c-5001-8da3-bc7d18a9ea75" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563787}}] :activity/title "The Power and Root Rules for Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562932}]}
